The migration is NOT "done" when the wasmer lane works — it is done when the two engines stop FEELING
like two engines. That vertical-integration moat is a prioritized, tracked thread: see
[[beam-wasm-kernel]] (epic #wb-sjg3). Phase 5 (host-cap bridge) is its FIRST brick — build it as the
unified Membrane's wasmer transport, not a one-off. Phase 6 (persistent session) feeds the density moat
(thousands of sub-MB cells on 1GB). Treat the kernel epic as part of THIS goal, not a follow-up.

## P0-robust — clean-package REBUILD recipe (VALIDATED toolchain, autonomous spike)

Confirmed the rebuild path end-to-end (components all present; the user's "use our clang" call is right —
the compiler isn't the blocker, the WASIX sysroot was). Findings:

- The prebuilt sharrattj packages work for direct pipes/chains/loops/glob/redirs/cat/ls/etc. over ABSOLUTE
  /work paths, but crash at TEARDOWN on the fork/exit path (`indirect call type mismatch`) — losing output
  on nested `$(…|…)` — and lack grep/sed/awk. Crash is on bash's FORK path → must rebuild bash (C), not
  just coreutils. So this is a C cross-compile, and we already have the compiler.

- TOOLCHAIN (all available on this host):
  * clang+wasm-ld: `/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/bin/clang` (targets wasm32 ✓), `/opt/homebrew/bin/wasm-ld`.
    (Apple `/usr/bin/clang` has NO wasm backend — must use brew llvm. Our in-image `clang.wasm` is a
    single-file `.work` lane, NOT suited to running ./configure && make — use a HOST clang to cross-compile.)
  * wasix-libc sysroot: github `wasix-org/wasix-libc` releases, `sysroot.tar.gz` (latest v2026-06-03.1, 5.7M).
    Unpacks to `…/sysroot/lib/wasm32-wasi/` with libc.a, libclang_rt.builtins-wasm32.a, libc++,
    libwasi-emulated-{process-clocks,getpid,mman}.a — everything needed.
  * runtime: wasmer 7.1 (`~/.wasmer/bin/wasmer`).

- WRINKLES hit (both standard, fixable):
  1. clang looks for builtins in its resource-dir as `lib/wasm32-unknown-wasi/libclang_rt.builtins.a`, but
     the sysroot ships `lib/wasm32-wasi/libclang_rt.builtins-wasm32.a` (wasi-sdk layout). FIX: symlink/copy
     the builtins into clang's expected resource-dir path, OR pass `-resource-dir`, OR (cleanest) use a
     wasi-sdk/wasix-sdk clang whose resource-dir already matches.
  2. fork-ish programs need `-D_WASI_EMULATED_PROCESS_CLOCKS -lwasi-emulated-process-clocks
     -lwasi-emulated-getpid` (getrusage/getpid emulation).

- REMAINING (the multi-hour focused build — next session, NOT a 3-min loop):
  a. Resolve the resource-dir/builtins path so `clang --target=wasm32-wasip1 --sysroot=$WASIX hello.c`
     links + runs on `wasmer run ./hello.wasm`. Then a `fork()` test → confirm NO teardown crash (proves
     the rebuilt-libc fixes it).
  b. Cross-compile **busybox** (best target: ONE binary = ash shell + grep/sed/awk + all applets, applets
     run in-process so fewer fork hops): `make defconfig` → set CC/AR/flags to the brew-clang+wasix-sysroot
     → `make`. Resolve the autoconf/feature wrinkles for the wasm target. (bash itself is an alternative but
     heavier autoconf; busybox is leaner + covers grep/sed/awk.)
  c. Package busybox.wasm as a wasmer package (wasmer.toml with `ash`+applet commands) OR run directly:
     `wasmer run busybox.wasm -- ash -c "<line>"`.
  d. Point `Nexus.WasmerCli` at the rebuilt package (config/const), verify nested `$(…|…)` + grep/sed/awk
     work + teardown crash gone; flip the integration toward flag-on once green.

- Then P1-image (vendor wasmer + busybox.wasm into the runtime image, BRANCH only — don't bloat main),
  P4 host-builtin WASIX-socket bridge, P5 persistent session, P6 security re-prove on wasmer.

## P0-robust UPDATE — toolchain VALIDATED (no-fork) + the fork wall (autonomous build session)

Built a working C→WASIX cross-compile with HOST tools (no in-image clang needed):
- clang `/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/bin/clang` `--target=wasm32-wasip1 --sysroot=$WASIX -resource-dir=$RD`
- $WASIX = wasix-org/wasix-libc `sysroot.tar.gz` (v2026-06-03.1). Bridge the triple-dir naming:
  `ln -s wasm32-wasi $WASIX/lib/wasm32-wasip1`. $RD = clang's real resource-dir `include` + the sysroot's
  `libclang_rt.builtins-wasm32.a` copied to `$RD/lib/wasm32-unknown-wasip1/libclang_rt.builtins.a`.
- thread/fork flags: `-matomics -mbulk-memory -mmutable-globals -pthread` + emulated libs
  (`-D_WASI_EMULATED_PROCESS_CLOCKS -lwasi-emulated-process-clocks -D_WASI_EMULATED_GETPID -lwasi-emulated-getpid`)
  + `-Wl,--shared-memory,--max-memory=4294967296`.
- RESULT: a no-fork C program (hello, filters) compiles → `wasmer run ./x.wasm` runs CLEAN (no teardown
  crash — the prebuilt-package bug is a PACKAGE artifact, gone in fresh builds). This unblocks building
  grep/sed/awk (single-process, NO fork) with this exact toolchain to fill the coreutils gap.

THE FORK WALL: a fork()-using C module compiles + imports `wasix_32v1.proc_fork` + futex/threads + shared
memory correctly, but at runtime `fork()` → exit 79 (no child/parent, no -1) on `wasmer run` (bare module
AND as a package). plain `wasm-opt --asyncify` did NOT fix it. WASIX proc_fork needs wasmer's exact build-
pipeline instrumentation (likely asyncify with `--pass-arg=asyncify-imports@wasix_32v1.*` or the newer
stack-switching + specific link) that cargo-wasix/the wasix-libc build encapsulate. The PREBUILT
sharrattj/bash forks fine (pipes produce output), so this only blocks a 100%-from-source bash. DECISION:
use prebuilt bash+coreutils (real bash, fork works) for the shell; build grep/sed/awk no-fork with the
toolchain above; treat from-source-bash (cracking proc_fork) as a later perfection track. Next recipe step
for that track: replicate cargo-wasix's exact post-processing (build a forking Rust prog via cargo-wasix,
diff its wasm vs ours for the missing asyncify/section).

## P0-robust BREAKTHROUGH — the exact WASIX build recipe (cargo-wasix decoded)

Decoded cargo-wasix's pipeline (it produces WORKING wasix modules — threading verified clean):
- Rust: target `wasm32-wasmer-wasi`, output `*.wasi.wasm`.
- POST-PROCESS (the missing keystone — NOT asyncify):
  `wasm-opt IN -O3 -o OUT --enable-bulk-memory --enable-threads --enable-reference-types --no-validation
   --translate-to-exnref --debuginfo`
  (cargo-wasix's own wasm-opt: ~/Library/Caches/cargo-wasix/0.1.28/wasm-opt/bin/wasm-opt)
- WASIX uses EXCEPTION-HANDLING → exnref for the fork/setjmp/stack mechanism (that's why plain
  --asyncify did nothing, and why my non-EH C fork hit exit-79).

FORK MECHANISM (evolving, the deep part): wasix-libc ships sysroot VARIANTS — plain, `-eh`, `-exnref-eh`.
In the EH sysroot, plain `fork()` is REMOVED (guard `!defined(__wasm_exception_handling__)`); EH builds use
`wasix_proc_snapshot()` + the exnref stack mechanism instead. So a from-source forking bash needs the EH/
exnref toolchain path end-to-end (compile -mexception-handling vs EH sysroot → wasm-opt --translate-to-exnref),
matching cargo-wasix. This is real, decoded, but multi-step C R&D.

PRAGMATIC PLAN (in progress): build clean uutils coreutils via cargo-wasix (Rust → works, fixes the
prebuilt 0.0.7 multi-call `sort` dispatch bug). Pair with prebuilt bash (forks) OR a from-source EH bash.
Newer prebuilts exist: bash up to 1.0.18, coreutils up to 1.0.16 (the cached 0.0.7 was ancient — re-pull).

## VERDICT (exhaustive autonomous build session) — vision PROVEN, "full" gated on UPSTREAM

Real bash + real coreutils DO run in wasm over /work via Wasmer/WASIX — the vision is real and the
integration (Nexus.WasmerCli + flag-gated routing + Elixir fallback) is built, tested, and on this branch.
What WORKS today via prebuilt `sharrattj/bash --use sharrattj/coreutils`: full bash GRAMMAR (loops,
conditionals, glob, redirects, simple pipes/$()) + ~8 coreutils applets (cat, ls, echo, wc, head, cut, tr,
uniq). That is already a genuine "wasm-linux sandbox with real bash" for those operations.

"FULL" (every applet, grep/sed/awk, nested $()|pipes, clean exit) is blocked by UPSTREAM wasix-ecosystem
immaturity — verified across FOUR independent paths this session:
1. Prebuilt packages: ancient (coreutils 0.0.7), incomplete command set (tail/sort/grep absent/broken
   dispatch), teardown `indirect call type mismatch`, nested-$()-pipe loses output.
2. From-source bash (C): the fork mechanism is mid-migration — wasix-libc EH sysroot REMOVES fork(), uses
   `wasix_proc_snapshot` + exception-handling→exnref (`wasm-opt --translate-to-exnref`). A from-source
   forking bash needs the full EH/exnref C pipeline end-to-end (decoded here, but multi-step, undocumented).
3. uutils via cargo-wasix: transitive dep `socket2 v0.6.3` has NO wasix port (no replacement).
4. uutils feat_Tier1: dep `crc-fast` build-script fails to link (`scrt1.o`).
cargo-wasix DOES produce working wasix modules for simple Rust (threading verified) — so the toolchain is
sound; the blockers are specific upstream crate/package ports + the in-flight fork model.

PATH TO 100% FULL (a dedicated effort / when upstream matures), in priority:
a. Build a minimal uutils subset that excludes socket2+crc-fast deps (curate features to cat/ls/sort/uniq/
   wc/head/tail/cut/tr/sed-less set; add `[patch.crates-io]` for libc/socket2; drop cksum/sum/hostname).
b. grep/sed/awk: build ripgrep/sd (Rust, cargo-wasix) or GNU grep/sed/gawk (C, the EH toolchain here).
c. From-source bash: the EH/exnref C pipeline (sysroot-eh + `-mexception-handling` + wasm-opt
   `--translate-to-exnref`), resolving the snapshot-fork API (`_Fork`/`wasix_proc_snapshot`).
d. Package bash + curated-coreutils + grep/sed/awk as ONE wasmer package; point Nexus.WasmerCli at it.
e. P1-image (vendor wasmer + the package, branch only), P4 host-builtin WASIX-socket bridge, P5 persistent
   session, P6 /work-only security re-prove on wasmer.

RECOMMENDATION: ship the flag-gated integration (real bash grammar + working applets, Elixir fallback) as
the milestone; pursue (a)-(d) as a focused follow-up (it's dependency surgery + the EH-bash build, not a
quick task). The toolchain + recipe are fully decoded here to make that follow-up tractable.

## DECISIONS (locked) — the two architectural forks

These were left open by the goal ("decide + execute"). Both are now decided, with rationale, so the
migration has a stable contract instead of an indefinite "maybe".

**DECISION 1 — pattern matching is PYTHON, not grep/sed/awk (Phase 4).** The agent env ships
`wasmer/python` over /work, which gives the agent regex search/replace/extract (re, fileinput, the
`python -c` one-liner idiom) — a strict superset of grep/sed/awk for an LLM that writes code. A
from-source grep/sed/awk for WASIX is blocked on the same upstream fork/EH-toolchain immaturity decoded
above (it is a multi-step C R&D track, not a quick win), and buys the agent nothing python doesn't
already cover. So: **python IS the documented pattern-matching path.** busybox/grep/sed/awk stays a
"later perfection" track (recipe (b) above), pursued only if a concrete need appears. The agent system
prompt should steer pattern work to python (`python -c "import re,sys;..."`).

**DECISION 2 — the COMPILER/COMPONENT lane stays on wasmtime/Wasmex PERMANENTLY (Phase 7).** This is a
deliberate, documented **dual-runtime** split, not a half-finished migration:

  * **wasmer (WASIX)** backs the AGENT SHELL — real bash + coreutils + python over /work. WASIX gives a
    POSIX-ish linux sandbox (fork/exec/pipes/fs). That is what the shell needs and wasmtime never gave us.
  * **wasmtime (via Wasmex)** backs the COMPILER/COMPONENT lane — Nexus.Sandbox, Nexus.Wasm(.Aot), the
    rust/c/zig/go/js compilers, Nexus.JsEngine, Nexus.ProcMacroHost, Nexus.SSR/Blitz, and the .work
    parser (Nexus.Toolchain). These depend on the **wasm COMPONENT MODEL + custom host imports** (WIT
    worlds, canonical-ABI lifting, Dock-supplied host functions). **WASIX does not provide the component
    model or arbitrary host imports** — it provides a syscall ABI. Porting this lane to wasmer would mean
    re-implementing component instantiation + custom-import binding on a runtime that has no first-class
    story for it: large, risky, and with **zero user-visible benefit** (the output wasm is identical).

  CONSEQUENCE: `vendor/wasmex` (~1.1GB) **stays** — it is not dead weight, it is the compiler lane's
  runtime. The goal's "only after this can vendor/wasmex be removed" is therefore resolved as **we do not
  remove it**; the dual-runtime is the permanent, correct architecture.

  This keeps the two lanes cleanly separated by what each runtime is actually good at: wasmer = a real
  OS-like sandbox for the agent; wasmtime/wasmex = a precise component host for the compiler.

  ### NOT sunk cost — the greenfield test, with dated evidence (verified 2026-06-23)

  The trap to avoid: "we already built the compiler lane on wasmex, so I guess we keep both." That is a
  sunk-cost justification and is INVALID. The only valid test: *a new team, zero code, starting today —
  which engine do they pick for each lane?* Sunk cost can't enter that question. Verified facts that
  decide it on MERIT:

  1. `wasmex` is **not our custom code** — it is a maintained third-party Elixir library (tessi/wasmex)
     that wraps **wasmtime** via a Rust NIF, and it already exposes the **component model + WIT** (a
     GenServer macro over component exports). "The Elixir version we built" is really "a maintained
     binding to the component-model REFERENCE runtime."
  2. **wasmer deliberately did NOT implement the component model** — it bet on **WASIX** as its extension
     mechanism. wasmtime is the component-model REFERENCE implementation; wasmer only "plans to align"
     (not first-class today). [sources: morphllm wasmtime-vs-wasmer 2026; component-model.bytecodealliance.org;
     hexdocs.pm/wasmex]
  3. The engines' OWN stated sweet spots map 1:1 onto our two lanes: wasmtime → "component-model
     composition for structured sandbox boundaries" (= compiler lane); wasmer → "WASIX
     networking / small binary / fast subprocesses" (= agent shell).
  4. There is **no first-class wasmer-Elixir-component path.** Choosing wasmer for the compiler lane means
     betting on "plans to align" AND building+maintaining our own NIF.

  GREENFIELD RESULT: compiler lane → **wasmtime/wasmex** (reference impl + maintained Elixir component
  binding); agent shell → **wasmer/WASIX** (only it has the POSIX process model real bash needs). The two
  engines made OPPOSITE design bets; our two jobs need opposite things; each job lands on the engine built
  for it. Migrating the compiler lane to wasmer would be **sunk-cost-in-reverse** — discarding a
  maintained reference-impl binding to chase an engine that chose not to support what we need.

  ANTI-ROT TRIGGER (so this can't silently become inertia): the dated expiry is wasmer's "plans to align
  with the component model." **RE-RUN the greenfield test when BOTH hold: (a) wasmer ships first-class
  component-model + custom-host-import support, and (b) a maintained wasmer-backed Elixir component binding
  exists.** Until then, dual-runtime wins on verified merit, not on what we already built.
